The magnetic field is unchanged: $\operatorname{curl}(A+
abla\chi)=\operatorname{curl}A+\operatorname{curl}
abla\chi=\operatorname{curl}A$, because $\operatorname{curl}$ is linear and $\operatorname{curl}
abla\chi=0$ for the $C^\infty$, hence $C^2$, function $\chi$.

The Lorenz divergence of the transformed pair vanishes: $\operatorname{div}(A+
abla\chi)+c^{-2}\partial_t(\varphi-\partial_t\chi)=\operatorname{div}A+\operatorname{div}
abla\chi+c^{-2}\partial_t\varphi-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\chi=G+\Delta\chi-c^{-2}\partial_t^2\chi=G-W\chi=0$ on $\mathbb R^3	imes(t_0,T)$, using $\Delta\chi=\operatorname{div}
abla\chi$ and the linearity of $\operatorname{div}$ and $\partial_t$.

The Hölder theorem [F2] applied with $\alpha=1/2$ gives $Nho\in C^2(\mathbb R^3)$ with $-\Delta (Nho)=ho$ pointwise; scaling $\varphi=\varepsilon_0^{-1}Nho$ and using linearity of the second coordinate derivatives gives $-\Delta\varphi=ho/\varepsilon_0$ pointwise, because $\varepsilon_0>0$ is a constant ([F4]). With $E:=-
abla\varphi$ one has $\operatorname{div}E=-\operatorname{div}
abla\varphi=-\Delta\varphi=ho/\varepsilon_0$ on $\mathbb R^3$ and $\operatorname{curl}E=-\operatorname{curl}
abla\varphi=0$ by [F4], since $\varphi$ is $C^2$.

